2. THE CONFOUND: accuracy split by switch vs stay trials
==============================================================================
   Consecutive trials use different hands ~76% of the time, and the
   previous trial's beta rebound is still large when the next cue
   arrives. A model riding that residue scores well when the hand
   SWITCHES and poorly when it REPEATS.

   Drift in attention/effort hits switch and repeat trials alike, so a
   gap HERE is carryover specifically, not slow drift.

  pooled-rest (primary)          overall 0.704 | switch 0.722 (n=1714) | stay 0.646 (n=509) | gap +0.075
  trial-wise pre-cue (injects)   overall 0.704 | switch 0.729 (n=1714) | stay 0.621 (n=509) | gap +0.108
  no baseline                    overall 0.726 | switch 0.741 (n=1714) | stay 0.674 (n=509) | gap +0.067

   Note the trial-wise arm has the LARGEST gap. Subtracting a pre-cue
   baseline makes carryover worse here, not better -- see
   figures/carryover_decay.png for why.

